In many countries, fragrance formulas are protected as trade secrets, meaning consumers often have no idea what they are spraying directly onto their skin and inhaling throughout the day.

Among the ingredients that frequently raise concerns in the scientific literature are phthalates, synthetic musks, certain UV filters, BHT, benzophenones, and fragrance allergens such as limonene, linalool, citral, and geraniol. Some of these compounds have been associated with endocrine disruption, skin sensitization, respiratory irritation, and environmental persistence.

Phthalates, particularly diethyl phthalate (DEP), have been repeatedly detected in fragrances and are often used to make scents last longer. Human and animal studies have linked some phthalates to hormone disruption and potential reproductive effects, which is why several countries have restricted specific phthalates in consumer products.

Synthetic musks are another concern. Research has found that certain musks can accumulate in human tissue and have been detected in blood, breast milk, and aquatic environments. Some studies suggest they may interfere with hormone signalling and increase the body’s overall chemical burden.

Many people are surprised to learn that fragrance allergens such as limonene and linalool are not necessarily problematic when freshly manufactured, but when exposed to air they can oxidise into compounds that are much more likely to trigger skin irritation and allergic reactions.

This matters because endocrine-disrupting chemicals do not act like traditional toxins. Instead, they may interfere with the body’s hormone signalling systems, which regulate metabolism, fertility, thyroid function, growth, mood, and development.
